WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.110
- Hello, everybody.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.330
Thank you so much for coming.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.330
We're very grateful for your presence

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.940
at this lecture this afternoon.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:15.810
I'm Kathy Rupp.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:19.080
I'm the director of the
Council on East Asian Studies,

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.540
and we will be seeing a short video

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:27.300
that Professor Eric Greene
will tell you about as well.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.903
He will be giving the
introduction of our honored guest.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:34.223
- Thank you, Kathy,

00:00:34.223 --> 00:00:37.050
for introducing the
introducer, as it were.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.340
Thank you all for coming.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:39.930
As Kathy said, I'm Eric Greene.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.330
I teach in the Department
of Religious Studies

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.340
focusing on Buddhism in China,

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.230
one of a couple faculty there

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.120
in the area of Buddhist studies.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.320
It's my great pleasure today

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.620
to introduce our speaker
the Venerable Jeongmok.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.870
To ensure our time is used efficiently,

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.960
I'm just gonna say a few
words before we get started.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.630
The Venerable Jeongmok stands
as one of the most eminent

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:04.290
senior female Buddhist
monastics in South Korea today.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.170
She was ordained in 1978,

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.290
and, oh, sorry, 1976, excuse me,

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:13.380
and completed her Bachelor's
Degree in Zen Studies

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.210
and then a master's degree in social work

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.340
from Dongguk University,

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.470
which is also the institution
where my colleague,

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:23.220
professor Hwansoo Kim had
his undergraduate degree.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.370
The Venerable Jeongmok has
served as a Buddhist chaplain

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.890
at the Seoul National University Hospital.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:32.100
And in 1997 she founded
the Small Love Foundation,

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.130
which offers financial support to families

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.410
with children battling cancer.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:40.260
She currently serves as the
abbess of Jeonggak Temple

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.300
in Central Seoul.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.570
She's renowned for her
pioneering work in Buddhist media

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:47.640
and initiated a daily radio program

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.670
with the Buddhist
Broadcasting Service in 1990,

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.010
and later established the
Una Broadcasting Service,

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.000
which is a YouTube channel

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.850
with nearly a quarter million subscribers.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.590
She was recently recognized as

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:01.530
one of the most influential
female Buddhist monastics

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.240
in South Korea today.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.310
She's the author of
several bestselling books

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.293
written in Korean, including,

00:02:08.705 --> 00:02:09.990
and I'm not sure where these translations

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:10.823
of the titles came from,

00:02:10.823 --> 00:02:12.307
but I'm sure they're very accurate,

00:02:12.307 --> 00:02:15.427
"Though the Snail is
Slow, It is Never Late,"

00:02:15.427 --> 00:02:17.910
"The More You Empty
Yourself, the More You Have,"

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:21.510
and "Even Flowers Have to
Make an Effort to Bloom."

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.810
She's today gonna share
her life story with us,

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.870
and her talk will be translated
by one of our MA students,

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.270
Hyunghoon Cho who is around somewhere.

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:31.110
- [Speaker] Oh, she's coming.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:32.910
- Who will come down when
that time, excellent.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:34.530
And following the presentation,

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.893
there'll be lots of time,
I hope, for your questions.

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:41.250
Before we commence with the main activity,

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.780
I wanna just express my gratitude

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.750
to the council staff on the
Council of East Asian Studies

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:48.690
for all their efforts
in making this possible.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:50.910
And before we welcome
the Venerable Jeongmok,

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.220
we are going to watch a
short two-minute video

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.130
that highlights some aspects of her work,

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:59.204
which I think will be queued
somehow miraculously now.

00:02:59.204 --> 00:03:00.564
(audience applauding)
There we go.

00:03:00.564 --> 00:03:01.397
(gentle music)

00:03:01.397 --> 00:03:04.748
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:03:04.748 --> 00:03:07.331
(gentle music)

00:03:09.642 --> 00:03:12.225
(upbeat music)

00:03:24.082 --> 00:03:26.665
(gentle music)

00:03:28.124 --> 00:03:32.791
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:03:45.898 --> 00:03:50.565
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:04:09.713 --> 00:04:12.713
(Buddhist clapping)

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:18.313
(gentle music)

00:04:19.193 --> 00:04:23.860
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:04:43.141 --> 00:04:46.308
(Buddists vocalizing)

00:04:53.061 --> 00:04:55.644
(upbeat music)

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:18.677
(audience applauding)

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:29.911
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:05:29.911 --> 00:05:32.744
(Jeongmok laughs)

00:05:43.671 --> 00:05:46.282
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:05:46.282 --> 00:05:47.513
- Hello, nice to meet you.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:50.823
- Hello, everyone.

00:05:58.217 --> 00:06:03.124
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:06:03.124 --> 00:06:03.957
(Jeongmok laughs)

00:06:03.957 --> 00:06:04.790
- Who made this video?

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:06.777
This is my first time seeing it.

00:06:06.777 --> 00:06:08.018
(audience laughs)

00:06:08.018 --> 00:06:11.430
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:14.553
- Actually, I'm not as great
as the video makes me seem.

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:18.570
- I'm so happy to be here.

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:21.930
This is the first time in my life

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:25.770
that I will been invited
to an American university.

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:29.100
So it is a very exciting time for me.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:34.100
From the time I was invited
a year ago until now,

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:36.180
I will been so excited

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:40.200
and looking forward to meeting all of you.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:45.183
And now I'm finally meeting you all.

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.303
I don't speak English very well.

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:51.900
I want to greet you in English.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:54.033
I practiced a few lines.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.680
So I decided to speak in Korean

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:01.113
and have someone translate for me.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:06.783
I talk to you in Korean from now on, yeah.

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:16.080
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:17.400
- [Translator] People actually call me

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:19.293
the mother of healing in Korea.

00:07:20.141 --> 00:07:23.430
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:24.570
I'm not even married,

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:27.568
and I've never had a child.
(audience laughs)

00:07:27.568 --> 00:07:29.672
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:07:29.672 --> 00:07:30.859
Do I look like a mother?

00:07:30.859 --> 00:07:32.164
(Jeongmok laughs)

00:07:32.164 --> 00:07:36.831
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:42.750
For a long time I've
wondered why people call me

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:43.983
the mother of healing.

00:07:45.046 --> 00:07:49.713
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:07:53.296 --> 00:07:54.938
Even when I was 30,

00:07:54.938 --> 00:07:58.701
a 50-year-old man once called me mother.

00:07:58.701 --> 00:08:02.160
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:05.733
He was crying, and he ran into
my arms like a young child.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:11.940
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:14.490
I hear some people call Yale University

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.380
with its 300-year history,

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:18.693
the mother of American universities.

00:08:20.014 --> 00:08:23.130
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:26.223
What does mother mean
to all forms of life?

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:32.497
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:36.390
Mothers are people we can truly trust,

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:39.273
trust the most after being
born into this world.

00:08:40.139 --> 00:08:44.591
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:08:44.591 --> 00:08:48.606
Moms are a symbol of
our faith in this world.

00:08:48.606 --> 00:08:52.380
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.533
People want to be protected
from a fearful world.

00:08:56.596 --> 00:09:01.596
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:05.283
Everyone wants to rely on
someone and be cared for.

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:09.720
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:12.390
I think people wanted to
find that sort of trust

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:13.623
and care in me.

00:09:14.878 --> 00:09:19.080
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:21.963
Maybe I reminded them of their mothers.

00:09:22.974 --> 00:09:25.380
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:27.903
That is probably why
they called me mother.

00:09:29.257 --> 00:09:33.150
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:36.270
In that respect, I am very
thankful for that title.

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:38.940
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:40.953
I feel very, very grateful.

00:09:42.953 --> 00:09:45.000
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:47.613
But I too feel fear sometimes.

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:52.110
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:55.413
And I have one particular
experience with fear.

00:09:57.866 --> 00:10:02.533
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:10:04.558 --> 00:10:09.308
In 1981, before most of the
students here were even born-

00:10:10.595 --> 00:10:15.330
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:17.580
I left to practice deep in the mountains,

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:19.833
and I was 21 years old at the time.

00:10:20.821 --> 00:10:25.050
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:27.780
The temple was a small shabby cabin,

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:29.943
deep in the mountains in the countryside.

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:35.837
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:40.380
I knew it was senior monk there,

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:43.110
so I set off for it with
my friend, a fellow monk.

00:10:43.110 --> 00:10:46.263
And the way to that temple
was incredibly rough.

00:10:47.272 --> 00:10:51.939
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:10:52.961 --> 00:10:53.794
There were no roads,

00:10:53.794 --> 00:10:56.280
and we had to go through a thick forest

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:57.993
and cross a deep river.

00:10:59.241 --> 00:11:03.908
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:07.860
Do you know those rivers

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:10.590
with strong churning
waters like a whirlpool?

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:13.293
We had to cross that to
get to the hermitage.

00:11:14.571 --> 00:11:17.280
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:21.213
So I took off my shoes and
threw it across the river.

00:11:21.213 --> 00:11:24.117
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:11:24.117 --> 00:11:27.345
And I took off my coat and
threw it across the river.

00:11:27.345 --> 00:11:29.850
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:33.838
I took off my sack and
threw it across the river.

00:11:33.838 --> 00:11:35.160
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:36.723
Had to jump and land.

00:11:38.771 --> 00:11:43.438
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:46.440
I had to land well,

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:49.353
sort of like Spider-Man
in today's Marvel movies.

00:11:50.711 --> 00:11:55.560
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:58.650
So back then, we fearlessly
set off for a place

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:01.814
where we could die while
crossing the river.

00:12:01.814 --> 00:12:06.481
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:11.220
But we arrived at the temple safely,

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:12.780
and my friend left two days later,

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:14.763
and I stayed there for about a month.

00:12:17.586 --> 00:12:22.253
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:12:23.892 --> 00:12:25.290
It was so deep in the mountains

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:27.333
that no one ever really came by.

00:12:28.532 --> 00:12:32.280
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:34.410
It was, in short, in the middle of nowhere

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:37.893
where it was hard to see even
one person for an entire year.

00:12:39.093 --> 00:12:43.760
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.040
And one day we ran out of rice,

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:50.493
so my senior monk had to leave
for the village to get some.

00:12:51.547 --> 00:12:55.380
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:56.850
She left early in the morning

00:12:56.850 --> 00:12:58.983
and told me she would be back by noon.

00:13:00.012 --> 00:13:02.638
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:13:02.638 --> 00:13:04.588
And I was left all alone in the temple.

00:13:05.693 --> 00:13:10.360
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:19.020
Noon came, and I was in the
kitchen scooping up some rice

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:21.000
for lunch when I suddenly felt

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:22.863
that there was someone behind me.

00:13:24.434 --> 00:13:29.101
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:33.210
You know, it was kind
of that eerie feeling,

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:35.973
and my nerves were very on edge.

00:13:37.078 --> 00:13:41.700
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:44.910
So I slowly turned around
as if nothing would matter,

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:46.083
and I looked back.

00:13:46.958 --> 00:13:51.720
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:53.730
And I saw three or four strangers,

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:56.763
very big men standing
in front of the door.

00:13:57.969 --> 00:14:01.410
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:04.110
Now, this was a place
where nobody ever came to,

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:06.603
but these people were snake catchers.

00:14:08.222 --> 00:14:12.900
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:14.047
They looked at me and said,

00:14:14.047 --> 00:14:16.677
"Sumim, please give us some water."

00:14:17.946 --> 00:14:22.613
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:14:30.086 --> 00:14:32.130
Now, there were no houses
around the hermitage.

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:35.880
So back then we used to drink
directly from a nearby stream.

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:37.740
So I gave them a gourd and told them

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:39.490
that's what they should do as well.

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:45.420
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:49.083
After drinking the water,
the men thanked me and left.

00:14:50.333 --> 00:14:55.000
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:14:57.810 --> 00:14:59.310
As soon as they disappeared,

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:01.980
I felt an immense wave
of fear come over me,

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:03.693
and I started trembling.

00:15:04.864 --> 00:15:09.531
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:15:18.467 --> 00:15:19.560
I ran into the Dharma Hall,

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:21.540
and I latched all the doors,

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:25.140
and I put a Korean style
spoon onto the door handle

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:25.983
to latch it.

00:15:26.977 --> 00:15:31.644
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:40.800
I was so afraid that I hid myself

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:42.600
in the innermost corner of the room

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:45.120
because I was scared of
my shadow being visible

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:47.913
if I was near the door,
which was made of paper.

00:15:48.927 --> 00:15:53.594
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:15:58.706 --> 00:16:01.500
I pulled the blanket
over myself and trembled.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:02.850
My heart was racing,

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:06.093
and I was so scared that I
couldn't think of anything.

00:16:07.377 --> 00:16:12.044
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:18.300
I forgot my hunger and the need to eat,

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:20.853
and I was completely
unaware of the time passing.

00:16:22.552 --> 00:16:27.219
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:36.300
Fear took over my entire body,

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:39.090
and the senior monk who
said she'd be back by now

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:40.440
still hadn't returned.

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:42.600
And I just stayed there shaking with fear.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:45.663
And before I knew it, the sun
had set, and it was evening.

00:16:47.273 --> 00:16:51.940
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:16:53.865 --> 00:16:55.440
It was 1981,

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:58.833
so we didn't have any
electricity in those mountains.

00:17:01.118 --> 00:17:02.940
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:05.793
And it started to get
dark outside the window.

00:17:06.903 --> 00:17:11.570
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:17.400
I had not done anything for half a day

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:19.830
because I was so overwhelmed with fear.

00:17:19.830 --> 00:17:22.383
But at that moment, a
thought occurred to me.

00:17:23.697 --> 00:17:28.364
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:33.843
It was like a light bulb
lighting up brightly in the dark.

00:17:36.103 --> 00:17:40.770
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:45.840
It had been a long time
since those people had gone.

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:48.453
So why am I still scared?

00:17:49.609 --> 00:17:51.780
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:17:51.780 --> 00:17:53.733
What is it that I'm afraid of?

00:17:54.997 --> 00:17:59.664
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:02.640
The moment the thought occurred,

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:04.593
my fear completely disappeared.

00:18:05.651 --> 00:18:08.985
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:18:08.985 --> 00:18:13.226
The world became clear as
if a veil had been lifted.

00:18:13.226 --> 00:18:17.893
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:22.530
It was then when I was 21-years-old

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:26.583
that I realized my fear was in
my thoughts and in my heart.

00:18:28.252 --> 00:18:33.060
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:34.140
To be more precise,

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:36.453
it was my mind that was creating the fear.

00:18:37.933 --> 00:18:42.600
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:56.130
Only then did I finally
pull back the blankets.

00:18:56.130 --> 00:18:58.530
I got up, and I saw the moonlight

00:18:58.530 --> 00:19:00.810
shining into the room through my window.

00:19:00.810 --> 00:19:03.543
And when I saw this, my mind felt at ease.

00:19:04.490 --> 00:19:09.157
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:19:12.359 --> 00:19:14.730
Do you remember that spoon I
latched to the door handles?

00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:16.920
I removed the spoon, opened the door,

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:18.213
and came out of the room.

00:19:19.564 --> 00:19:24.231
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:38.220
How can I even explain this feeling?

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:39.900
This was in the middle of summer,

00:19:39.900 --> 00:19:41.790
but when I went out into the yard,

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:43.470
the moonlight was so white,

00:19:43.470 --> 00:19:46.683
it was as if white snow had
covered the entire yard.

00:19:47.953 --> 00:19:52.620
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:57.420
There was also a pine tree in the yard,

00:19:57.420 --> 00:19:59.733
and its shadow was swaying in the wind.

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:05.907
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:20:11.358 --> 00:20:14.610
And so I wrote a few lines about
what I felt in that moment,

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:16.650
which later became the lyrics to a song

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:18.387
called "Wendy Hermitage."

00:20:19.866 --> 00:20:24.533
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:28.500
Wendy Hermitage, you
probably didn't even know

00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:29.763
this song existed.

00:20:31.677 --> 00:20:32.790
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:20:32.790 --> 00:20:34.023
Of course not, right?

00:20:35.652 --> 00:20:40.319
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:50.490
The funny thing is this song
became the most popular song

00:20:50.490 --> 00:20:53.190
among Korean Buddhists for 30 years.

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:54.423
Isn't that surprising?

00:20:55.866 --> 00:20:57.420
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:20:57.420 --> 00:20:59.913
Honestly, I don't really
know how that happened.

00:21:01.756 --> 00:21:06.423
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:21:10.890 --> 00:21:13.940
The lyrics of this song
capture the fear that froze me

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:17.160
in my youth, as well as
the realization that came

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:19.160
in the moment that I broke free from it.

00:21:20.278 --> 00:21:24.945
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:21:31.710 --> 00:21:34.170
It was the first time
that I truly understood

00:21:34.170 --> 00:21:36.360
that I am the one creating the fear,

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:39.090
and that I am the only
one who can recognize

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:40.473
and overcome this.

00:21:42.291 --> 00:21:45.660
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:21:45.660 --> 00:21:47.790
This is the first few lines of the song.

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:48.913
Should I sing it?

00:21:49.918 --> 00:21:54.224
(Jeongmok laughs)
(audience applauding)

00:21:54.224 --> 00:21:58.807
(Jeongmok singing in Korean)

00:22:34.079 --> 00:22:39.079
(Jeongmok singing in
Korean continues)

00:22:44.621 --> 00:22:48.246
(audience applauding)

00:22:48.246 --> 00:22:52.913
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:22:56.730 --> 00:22:59.310
So I just sang a first
few lines of the song,

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:02.763
and I know that nowadays this
may not be your cup of tea.

00:23:04.824 --> 00:23:09.491
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:23:10.729 --> 00:23:12.630
We think we know our own minds,

00:23:12.630 --> 00:23:15.093
but in reality we really don't.

00:23:15.929 --> 00:23:20.490
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:24.213
And that is why I wrote these
words, I can't draw my mind.

00:23:25.130 --> 00:23:27.570
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:23:27.570 --> 00:23:29.613
And perhaps the same goes for fear.

00:23:31.356 --> 00:23:36.023
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:23:38.130 --> 00:23:41.430
Fear is an emotion that
we feel instinctively

00:23:41.430 --> 00:23:45.542
in the process of caring for
and protecting ourselves.

00:23:45.542 --> 00:23:50.209
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:57.030
You may have heard of this,

00:23:57.030 --> 00:23:59.910
but in neuroscience, the area of the brain

00:23:59.910 --> 00:24:02.880
that triggers the flight
response in our moments of fear

00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:04.293
is called the amygdala.

00:24:05.265 --> 00:24:08.400
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:24:08.400 --> 00:24:11.703
We need this in order to take
care of ourselves properly.

00:24:12.717 --> 00:24:17.384
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:22.773
However, when this fear becomes
excessive, it overwhelms us.

00:24:23.645 --> 00:24:26.670
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:28.893
Then we see nothing but fear.

00:24:30.127 --> 00:24:35.127
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:24:35.340 --> 00:24:36.960
When the moment of fear passes,

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:40.434
there is no need to
dwell on it any longer.

00:24:40.434 --> 00:24:45.101
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:56.700
When a gazelle sees another gazelle

00:24:56.700 --> 00:24:58.800
being eaten by a cheetah,

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:01.080
it may be very shocked in that moment,

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:02.970
but after that moment passes,

00:25:02.970 --> 00:25:05.883
it will not dwell on that
fear, it will move on.

00:25:06.965 --> 00:25:11.632
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:15.630
The same is true of good things as well

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:17.700
because we can't remain perpetually

00:25:17.700 --> 00:25:19.293
in a state of excitement.

00:25:20.586 --> 00:25:25.253
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:25:26.730 --> 00:25:29.430
Whether it is fear or something we like,

00:25:29.430 --> 00:25:32.853
what we need is something
called attention recovery.

00:25:33.872 --> 00:25:38.539
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:42.120
When something happens,

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:44.250
you should focus your attention on it,

00:25:44.250 --> 00:25:47.523
but when it's resolved, you
should withdraw that attention.

00:25:48.515 --> 00:25:53.182
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:56.310
But many people find this difficult

00:25:56.310 --> 00:25:59.730
recovering your attention
from if whatever is

00:25:59.730 --> 00:26:02.553
making you afraid or
whatever is making you happy.

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:08.480
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:26:08.650 --> 00:26:10.620
For example, when you
feel a negative emotion

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:11.850
towards someone else,

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:15.143
you will continue to
pour your energy into it.

00:26:15.143 --> 00:26:19.810
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:26:23.130 --> 00:26:24.960
You think about it when you get home

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:26.850
and sometimes even dream about it,

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:29.613
and that is where you
pour all your energy into.

00:26:30.678 --> 00:26:34.440
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:36.210
If you don't recover your attention,

00:26:36.210 --> 00:26:38.463
you'll continue to drain your energy.

00:26:39.681 --> 00:26:43.386
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:26:43.386 --> 00:26:45.210
And that's probably
why people get so tired

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:46.533
and so easily lately.

00:26:48.001 --> 00:26:52.668
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:55.590
When something bad happens,

00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:58.713
there's no use in asking
why this happened.

00:26:59.980 --> 00:27:04.230
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:27:04.230 --> 00:27:07.113
Let's say that a tornado comes
and blows your house away.

00:27:08.430 --> 00:27:13.097
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:17.760
No one can answer the question

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:20.040
of why your house was blown away

00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:22.413
while the house next door was fine.

00:27:23.611 --> 00:27:28.278
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:27:32.736 --> 00:27:35.550
It's important not to dwell
on why these things happen,

00:27:35.550 --> 00:27:38.190
but to think about what
steps you should take next

00:27:38.190 --> 00:27:41.193
and focus your attention
on resolving the situation.

00:27:42.158 --> 00:27:45.811
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:27:45.811 --> 00:27:47.911
In this respect, life is just like a game.

00:27:48.876 --> 00:27:53.543
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:27:56.550 --> 00:27:59.760
We sometimes ask questions
like, why was I born,

00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:02.310
and blame our parents or our society

00:28:02.310 --> 00:28:04.083
for things that happened to us.

00:28:05.038 --> 00:28:09.089
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:28:09.089 --> 00:28:12.387
But there is no answer to the
question of why we are born.

00:28:13.489 --> 00:28:17.160
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:28:17.160 --> 00:28:20.163
The moment we are born,
the game of life begins.

00:28:20.997 --> 00:28:24.840
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:26.100
Once the game has started,

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:29.103
you need to live and act
by the rules of the game.

00:28:30.010 --> 00:28:34.677
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:28:37.519 --> 00:28:40.500
All we can do is think
about how to do our best

00:28:40.500 --> 00:28:42.333
with what we've been already given.

00:28:43.611 --> 00:28:48.278
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:28:56.249 --> 00:28:57.510
If we enter the playing field,

00:28:57.510 --> 00:29:00.000
and the ball comes
flying in our direction,

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:03.030
we need to catch it quickly
and pass it on to someone else

00:29:03.030 --> 00:29:04.800
instead of asking questions like,

00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:06.813
why is the ball flying towards me?

00:29:07.797 --> 00:29:11.220
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:29:11.220 --> 00:29:12.960
We can't be asking questions like,

00:29:12.960 --> 00:29:14.960
what am I supposed to do with this ball?

00:29:16.316 --> 00:29:19.860
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:29:19.860 --> 00:29:22.830
So when the game begins,
don't just look around,

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:23.943
start moving.

00:29:25.302 --> 00:29:30.302
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:29:30.557 --> 00:29:32.220
Depending on the stage of your life,

00:29:32.220 --> 00:29:35.823
the rules and the playing
grounds will continue to change.

00:29:36.677 --> 00:29:40.380
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:29:40.380 --> 00:29:44.163
And we just need to continue
playing according to our rules.

00:29:45.194 --> 00:29:48.450
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:49.950
Just because we lose one game

00:29:49.950 --> 00:29:51.603
doesn't mean your life is over.

00:29:52.474 --> 00:29:55.710
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:29:55.710 --> 00:29:59.403
You will always get another
chance, and that is life.

00:30:00.837 --> 00:30:05.160
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:30:05.160 --> 00:30:08.733
From the moment we are
born, we grow as we age.

00:30:09.607 --> 00:30:12.750
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:30:12.750 --> 00:30:15.093
But there are two paths in life.

00:30:16.119 --> 00:30:20.580
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:30:20.580 --> 00:30:24.453
One is a horizontal path, and
the other is a vertical path.

00:30:25.305 --> 00:30:29.972
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:36.180
The horizontal path is a life
that progresses laterally

00:30:36.180 --> 00:30:39.720
as one ages through
infancy, adolescence, youth,

00:30:39.720 --> 00:30:41.613
adulthood, and old age.

00:30:42.538 --> 00:30:47.205
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:30:51.030 --> 00:30:53.160
The word horizontal here means that

00:30:53.160 --> 00:30:55.560
although you may grow older in age,

00:30:55.560 --> 00:30:59.043
you remain on the same level,
only moving from side to side.

00:31:00.530 --> 00:31:05.197
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:31:07.650 --> 00:31:12.000
This path is living a life full
of sensuality and distrust,

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:13.743
desire and anger.

00:31:15.078 --> 00:31:19.745
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:31:26.892 --> 00:31:29.220
On the other hand, the
vertical path is a life

00:31:29.220 --> 00:31:31.560
in which you grow by actively leaping,

00:31:31.560 --> 00:31:34.830
uproot through infancy,
adolescence, youth,

00:31:34.830 --> 00:31:36.633
adulthood and old age.

00:31:37.519 --> 00:31:40.530
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:31:40.530 --> 00:31:44.043
And this path becomes filled
with the exploration of life.

00:31:45.254 --> 00:31:50.254
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:31:50.707 --> 00:31:52.680
In this kind of life, you get older,

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:54.300
and you're getting more experience,

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:56.253
and you are not easily swayed.

00:31:57.340 --> 00:32:00.900
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:32:00.900 --> 00:32:02.820
Rather, a life becomes more beautiful

00:32:02.820 --> 00:32:04.323
with each passing year.

00:32:05.761 --> 00:32:09.150
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:32:09.150 --> 00:32:12.273
Just because you lose once
doesn't mean everything is over.

00:32:13.301 --> 00:32:16.500
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:32:16.500 --> 00:32:20.648
You'll always get another
chance even if this one passes.

00:32:20.648 --> 00:32:22.050
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:32:22.050 --> 00:32:23.223
That is life.

00:32:24.407 --> 00:32:26.520
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:32:26.520 --> 00:32:28.967
So believe in yourself.

00:32:28.967 --> 00:32:30.840
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:32:30.840 --> 00:32:33.510
And you also need to love yourself.

00:32:33.510 --> 00:32:38.310
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:32:38.310 --> 00:32:41.733
Don't forget that you are the
main character of this life.

00:32:43.218 --> 00:32:45.510
- Remember, life is on your side.

00:32:45.510 --> 00:32:48.810
Thank you for listening my story.

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:50.343
Thank you so much.

00:32:51.258 --> 00:32:54.425
(audience applauding)

00:33:02.001 --> 00:33:06.668
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:33:20.242 --> 00:33:24.909
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:33:26.351 --> 00:33:29.110
- [Speaker] Any questions
from the audience?

00:33:29.110 --> 00:33:31.943
(Jeongmok laughs)

00:33:34.204 --> 00:33:35.850
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:33:35.850 --> 00:33:38.926
- Should we end this if there
are no further questions?

00:33:38.926 --> 00:33:39.759
(audience laughs)

00:33:39.759 --> 00:33:40.592
- Yes.

00:33:44.792 --> 00:33:46.740
- [Audience Member] For
people who aren't monastics

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:48.990
but who are practicing Buddhism,

00:33:48.990 --> 00:33:50.970
how do you think we can most meaningfully

00:33:50.970 --> 00:33:52.590
make that a part of our life

00:33:52.590 --> 00:33:55.710
when we have so many distractions

00:33:55.710 --> 00:33:59.160
and so many pressures put upon us to,

00:33:59.160 --> 00:34:03.090
especially in like a university,
keep up with other statuses

00:34:03.090 --> 00:34:08.090
of status, and career, and all of that.

00:34:10.418 --> 00:34:15.251
(translator speaking in Korean)

00:34:38.496 --> 00:34:43.163
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:34:49.498 --> 00:34:54.165
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:35:09.270 --> 00:35:11.550
- So I don't think there's a separate path

00:35:11.550 --> 00:35:13.470
that practitioners need to take.

00:35:13.470 --> 00:35:16.290
It doesn't matter if you're
in a temple or a university.

00:35:16.290 --> 00:35:20.130
What matters is what you do
in your current situation,

00:35:20.130 --> 00:35:22.143
what you decide your mind.

00:35:32.790 --> 00:35:37.020
- I was very captivated by your thoughts

00:35:37.020 --> 00:35:41.703
about attention control and retaining,

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:45.093
regaining attention control.

00:35:46.198 --> 00:35:49.710
And I think it's particularly
important in digital times

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:54.710
when we are so much
engaged and being engaged

00:35:54.990 --> 00:35:59.990
into giving away our attention
in this economy of attention.

00:36:01.680 --> 00:36:05.040
For those who do not
practice, just ordinary us,

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:08.850
are there any ways you could tell us to

00:36:08.850 --> 00:36:12.060
function better under these circumstances?

00:36:13.619 --> 00:36:18.452
(translator speaking in Korean)

00:36:29.082 --> 00:36:32.730
- Good question.
(Jeongmok laughs)

00:36:32.730 --> 00:36:37.397
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:37:11.040 --> 00:37:13.050
- So you don't need to practice Buddhism

00:37:13.050 --> 00:37:15.480
in order to control yourself, your life.

00:37:15.480 --> 00:37:17.610
We all want to live a better life.

00:37:17.610 --> 00:37:20.550
And the reason why we
consume so much information

00:37:20.550 --> 00:37:22.740
is because we want to know more.

00:37:22.740 --> 00:37:24.630
And what's really important here

00:37:24.630 --> 00:37:28.110
is to turn your attention
to your inner self.

00:37:28.110 --> 00:37:30.930
We need to take, we need
to discard of the labels,

00:37:30.930 --> 00:37:32.910
like oh, Buddhist practitioner.

00:37:32.910 --> 00:37:35.070
Whether we are Buddhist
practitioners or not,

00:37:35.070 --> 00:37:37.860
the important thing is to turn

00:37:37.860 --> 00:37:40.770
and look at your inside, your inner self.

00:37:40.770 --> 00:37:45.770
- For example, computer.

00:37:47.460 --> 00:37:52.127
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:38:10.860 --> 00:38:13.680
- So when you're looking at
information on a computer,

00:38:13.680 --> 00:38:18.150
try to be aware of the very
act of looking on the screen

00:38:18.150 --> 00:38:21.150
and think about who is looking at what,

00:38:21.150 --> 00:38:23.610
the subject and the object of the gaze.

00:38:23.610 --> 00:38:27.060
And try to think, try to
focus on what exactly is it

00:38:27.060 --> 00:38:30.153
that I want to gain from this information.

00:38:31.772 --> 00:38:32.910
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:38:32.910 --> 00:38:35.809
Do you play a lot of online games?

00:38:35.809 --> 00:38:36.892
(audience laughs)

00:38:36.892 --> 00:38:39.828
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:38:39.828 --> 00:38:41.213
Then you have nothing to worry about?

00:38:42.810 --> 00:38:43.643
- Yes.

00:38:46.890 --> 00:38:51.690
- I've been practicing
Buddhism for a few years,

00:38:51.690 --> 00:38:53.220
not as much as I would like to,

00:38:53.220 --> 00:38:57.390
but I have found it very important

00:38:57.390 --> 00:38:59.130
to be very present in the moment.

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:03.153
And I found that active
practice takes effort.

00:39:04.350 --> 00:39:08.250
And I very much appreciate
what you said in your story,

00:39:08.250 --> 00:39:12.930
but in today's society, when
we have the conflicts we have

00:39:12.930 --> 00:39:17.930
with Israel and Hamas, I want
to be present in the moment,

00:39:18.540 --> 00:39:22.320
but I also wanna respect what is happening

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:23.310
and to consider that.

00:39:23.310 --> 00:39:26.013
So how do you reconcile those two?

00:39:27.360 --> 00:39:28.920
You know, I obviously have to live

00:39:28.920 --> 00:39:30.900
and go throughout the day,

00:39:30.900 --> 00:39:33.093
but I feel for the people so much.

00:39:35.665 --> 00:39:40.498
(translator speaking in Korean)

00:39:55.620 --> 00:39:57.363
- Oh yes, yes, yes.

00:39:58.470 --> 00:39:59.303
Yeah.

00:40:01.440 --> 00:40:05.043
May the victims rest in peace, yeah.

00:40:10.667 --> 00:40:15.667
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:40:15.960 --> 00:40:18.513
- War is not something
that should ever happen.

00:40:20.210 --> 00:40:23.040
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:26.043
And we have been through so
many wars throughout history.

00:40:26.890 --> 00:40:29.700
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:40:29.700 --> 00:40:31.683
But we still haven't learned from it.

00:40:32.862 --> 00:40:34.590
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:40:34.590 --> 00:40:37.173
It's because of foolishness and ignorance.

00:40:39.117 --> 00:40:43.784
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:40:46.950 --> 00:40:51.240
Even if there are personal or
national benefits through war,

00:40:51.240 --> 00:40:54.599
that nothing is more important than life.

00:40:54.599 --> 00:40:58.650
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:40:58.650 --> 00:41:01.053
We won't be able to
stop war through anger.

00:41:02.410 --> 00:41:05.460
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:41:05.460 --> 00:41:08.610
Being angry and wanting revenge will,

00:41:08.610 --> 00:41:11.848
you can't stop it with more anger.

00:41:11.848 --> 00:41:15.270
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:41:15.270 --> 00:41:17.463
You can only end anger with compassion.

00:41:20.210 --> 00:41:24.877
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:41:27.120 --> 00:41:32.120
But when war is happening, we
all feel pain for the victims.

00:41:32.531 --> 00:41:35.280
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:41:35.280 --> 00:41:37.863
But what we really need to
focus on in that moment-

00:41:38.996 --> 00:41:41.970
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:41:41.970 --> 00:41:44.130
We need to be focusing on what we can do

00:41:44.130 --> 00:41:45.693
in our current situation.

00:41:46.637 --> 00:41:51.304
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:41:52.440 --> 00:41:55.950
Beethoven continued to compose
music during World War II,

00:41:55.950 --> 00:41:58.902
even when bullets were
flying over his head.

00:41:58.902 --> 00:42:02.565
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:42:02.565 --> 00:42:03.570
And war reporters know

00:42:03.570 --> 00:42:05.580
that there is a very real chance of death,

00:42:05.580 --> 00:42:08.409
but they're still there
in the thick of it.

00:42:08.409 --> 00:42:13.076
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:42:16.500 --> 00:42:19.170
No matter all the terrible
things that happen in this world,

00:42:19.170 --> 00:42:22.890
we need to be aware of where
we are and what we need to do.

00:42:22.890 --> 00:42:24.540
- It's okay.

00:42:24.540 --> 00:42:25.373
Thank you.

00:42:26.436 --> 00:42:28.236
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:42:28.236 --> 00:42:30.090
- Thank you for sharing.

00:42:30.090 --> 00:42:30.923
- Okay.

00:42:40.350 --> 00:42:42.701
- Thank you so much for your time today.

00:42:42.701 --> 00:42:44.070
I think we all really appreciate it.

00:42:44.070 --> 00:42:47.400
My question was, in your experience,

00:42:47.400 --> 00:42:50.433
what is the most effective
way to learn about Buddhism?

00:42:53.099 --> 00:42:57.932
(translator speaking in Korean)

00:43:03.157 --> 00:43:05.331
- Okay.
(Jeongmok laughs)

00:43:05.331 --> 00:43:06.360
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:43:06.360 --> 00:43:08.010
- Are you interested in Buddhism?

00:43:09.572 --> 00:43:11.355
- Do you like (Jeongmok
speaking in Korean)

00:43:11.355 --> 00:43:13.035
(Jeongmok laughs)
- Do you like Buddhism?

00:43:13.035 --> 00:43:13.868
- Buddhism.

00:43:13.868 --> 00:43:14.701
Yes, thank you.

00:43:16.335 --> 00:43:21.335
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:43:21.360 --> 00:43:22.950
- It's very simple to,

00:43:22.950 --> 00:43:26.040
the easiest way of knowing
Buddhism in your everyday life

00:43:26.040 --> 00:43:27.838
is very simple.

00:43:27.838 --> 00:43:31.380
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:43:31.380 --> 00:43:34.443
Make others happy the
way you want to be happy.

00:43:35.550 --> 00:43:39.270
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:43:39.270 --> 00:43:41.040
Just like you want to avoid pain,

00:43:41.040 --> 00:43:43.740
avoid inflicting pain on others.

00:43:43.740 --> 00:43:47.324
- That's it.
(audience laughs)

00:43:47.324 --> 00:43:50.491
(audience applauding)

00:43:51.360 --> 00:43:52.800
- Thank you, hi.

00:43:52.800 --> 00:43:53.700
First of all, thank you so much

00:43:53.700 --> 00:43:55.890
for your lovely talk and your story.

00:43:55.890 --> 00:43:58.140
I just wanted to ask a question about,

00:43:58.140 --> 00:44:00.780
we covered a little bit about fear,

00:44:00.780 --> 00:44:03.183
and especially here in the West, we have,

00:44:04.110 --> 00:44:06.540
whether it's the media industry,

00:44:06.540 --> 00:44:10.380
there's like a profit
profiting off of fear.

00:44:10.380 --> 00:44:14.126
There's a utilization of
fear systematically to,

00:44:14.126 --> 00:44:14.959
whether it's to divide people

00:44:14.959 --> 00:44:19.959
or to, you know, fund or create
support for wars, you know,

00:44:20.010 --> 00:44:23.220
get in the face of this
systemic rise of fear,

00:44:23.220 --> 00:44:25.080
not only individually within people,

00:44:25.080 --> 00:44:27.480
but like from the institutions itself.

00:44:27.480 --> 00:44:30.000
How do you think we should
take a more systematic approach

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:32.553
to tackle this growing rampant fear?

00:44:34.525 --> 00:44:39.442
(translator speaking in Korean)

00:44:46.972 --> 00:44:49.908
- Oh, very difficult question.

00:44:49.908 --> 00:44:53.293
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:44:53.293 --> 00:44:55.740
- I mean, I can't do much about it.

00:44:55.740 --> 00:44:56.763
- Yeah, but.

00:45:00.106 --> 00:45:04.773
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:45:08.280 --> 00:45:11.010
- What I would like to
say is don't be too swayed

00:45:11.010 --> 00:45:14.529
by all the information that
you receive from the outside.

00:45:14.529 --> 00:45:17.714
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:45:17.714 --> 00:45:19.440
The outside is just the outside,

00:45:19.440 --> 00:45:22.138
whether it's the media or the news.

00:45:22.138 --> 00:45:23.700
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:45:23.700 --> 00:45:26.343
You need to be firm and not swayed.

00:45:27.343 --> 00:45:31.290
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:45:31.290 --> 00:45:35.313
Elephants don't fall even when
they receive a lot of arrows.

00:45:36.504 --> 00:45:40.890
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:45:40.890 --> 00:45:43.173
Your inner self, you need to be firm.

00:45:44.700 --> 00:45:49.367
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:45:54.420 --> 00:45:56.700
So even if there is a
war going on outside,

00:45:56.700 --> 00:46:00.120
if you maintain peace and
calmness within yourself,

00:46:00.120 --> 00:46:02.400
and everybody sort of contributes to that,

00:46:02.400 --> 00:46:05.343
we will be able to
contribute to the world.

00:46:06.517 --> 00:46:08.550
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:46:08.550 --> 00:46:10.500
Thank you for thinking about the world.

00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:20.190
- [Audience Member] Hi,
thank you very much.

00:46:20.190 --> 00:46:21.450
I'm gonna relay this in English

00:46:21.450 --> 00:46:23.603
'cause I don't know how
to say this in Korean.

00:46:26.010 --> 00:46:29.850
I'm really captivated by the
concept of attention reset

00:46:29.850 --> 00:46:31.770
and attention recovery.

00:46:31.770 --> 00:46:35.670
But really what are the
best ways to apply that

00:46:35.670 --> 00:46:38.670
in moments of joy and happiness?

00:46:38.670 --> 00:46:41.340
I think that's something that
I'm still trying to understand

00:46:41.340 --> 00:46:43.980
is kind of, you know, in moments of fear,

00:46:43.980 --> 00:46:48.980
moments of, you know, just
of trouble and challenges,

00:46:49.230 --> 00:46:50.854
I understand,

00:46:50.854 --> 00:46:52.140
and I think like just
it's in our nature to,

00:46:52.140 --> 00:46:54.960
at least it's in my nature to, you know,

00:46:54.960 --> 00:46:57.180
want to have apply that
attention recovery.

00:46:57.180 --> 00:46:59.820
But I'm just trying to figure
out how I can apply that

00:46:59.820 --> 00:47:01.473
in moments of joy as well.

00:47:03.175 --> 00:47:08.008
(translator speaking in foreign laughing)

00:47:10.852 --> 00:47:11.958
(Jeongmok laughs)

00:47:11.958 --> 00:47:16.625
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:47:18.330 --> 00:47:22.533
- When we're happy and joyful,
we want to stay that way.

00:47:23.742 --> 00:47:28.409
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:47:29.700 --> 00:47:31.620
But in reality, if you think about it,

00:47:31.620 --> 00:47:35.795
it's harder to recover your
attention in moments of fear.

00:47:35.795 --> 00:47:37.800
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:47:37.800 --> 00:47:42.772
And if you're unlucky, It
could also lead to death.

00:47:42.772 --> 00:47:46.650
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:47:46.650 --> 00:47:49.740
And even joyful things,
things that make you happy,

00:47:49.740 --> 00:47:53.043
if it's in excess, it
could also lead to death.

00:47:53.984 --> 00:47:58.050
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:47:58.050 --> 00:48:01.627
So in Buddhism we practice mindfulness.

00:48:01.627 --> 00:48:04.050
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:48:04.050 --> 00:48:08.236
And the reason for that is
to avoid these two extremes.

00:48:08.236 --> 00:48:12.903
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:48:14.040 --> 00:48:15.450
There's no good no bad.

00:48:15.450 --> 00:48:17.973
If we put an end to those two extremes-

00:48:18.874 --> 00:48:23.122
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:48:23.122 --> 00:48:26.250
We'll be able to know that joy and fear,

00:48:26.250 --> 00:48:27.693
neither are eternal.

00:48:37.680 --> 00:48:39.480
- I really appreciated that analogy

00:48:39.480 --> 00:48:40.770
about life being a game,

00:48:40.770 --> 00:48:42.660
and when you get thrown a ball,

00:48:42.660 --> 00:48:43.950
you just have to catch it quickly

00:48:43.950 --> 00:48:45.780
and throw it to someone else.

00:48:45.780 --> 00:48:47.490
But practically, what does that mean?

00:48:47.490 --> 00:48:50.190
I think it's very easy as
college students especially

00:48:50.190 --> 00:48:52.560
to get stuck in the question of like,

00:48:52.560 --> 00:48:54.060
what do I do with this ball?

00:48:54.060 --> 00:48:56.673
So how do we know to just throw it?

00:48:57.952 --> 00:49:02.785
(translator speaking in Korean)

00:49:08.007 --> 00:49:08.910
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:49:08.910 --> 00:49:10.310
- Are you a college student?

00:49:11.244 --> 00:49:12.077
- Okay.

00:49:15.098 --> 00:49:17.790
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:49:17.790 --> 00:49:21.123
- So when people are born, we
have 800 bones in our body.

00:49:22.485 --> 00:49:26.340
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:49:26.340 --> 00:49:28.500
And as we grow, they fuse into,

00:49:28.500 --> 00:49:32.593
fuse with each other to form
206 bones by the age of 30.

00:49:33.727 --> 00:49:38.394
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:49:41.640 --> 00:49:45.480
So for our brains, between
the ages of 25 to 30,

00:49:45.480 --> 00:49:48.000
the neurons, our neurons will be covered

00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:49.713
by this thing called myelin.

00:49:51.030 --> 00:49:54.450
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:49:54.450 --> 00:49:56.520
And you haven't gotten to that stage yet.

00:49:56.520 --> 00:49:57.723
You're still growing.

00:49:58.757 --> 00:50:00.330
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:50:00.330 --> 00:50:04.129
Don't be afraid to
explore and make mistakes.

00:50:04.129 --> 00:50:05.984
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:50:05.984 --> 00:50:06.817
(audience laughs)

00:50:06.817 --> 00:50:08.770
Go through a lot of
trials and tribulations.

00:50:08.770 --> 00:50:11.310
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:50:11.310 --> 00:50:15.462
The most interesting in
life is about the trials.

00:50:15.462 --> 00:50:19.080
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:50:19.080 --> 00:50:20.610
Look at sports athletes.

00:50:20.610 --> 00:50:22.620
They go through a lot of trouble,

00:50:22.620 --> 00:50:25.593
and then before finally
winning that gold medal.

00:50:26.675 --> 00:50:29.220
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:50:29.220 --> 00:50:32.500
Don't be afraid of the
waves that come at you.

00:50:32.500 --> 00:50:36.360
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:50:36.360 --> 00:50:37.350
When you're afraid,

00:50:37.350 --> 00:50:40.623
tell yourself that it's okay
and everything will pass.

00:50:41.489 --> 00:50:45.567
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:50:45.567 --> 00:50:46.770
And this is something that I mentioned

00:50:46.770 --> 00:50:48.750
at the Buddhist Student Community event

00:50:48.750 --> 00:50:50.013
that we had yesterday.

00:50:50.880 --> 00:50:54.000
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:57.033
In America we have the
Yosemite National Park.

00:50:58.060 --> 00:51:02.727
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:51:03.720 --> 00:51:07.170
And when I went there 20 years
ago, there were so many trees

00:51:07.170 --> 00:51:10.443
that were 2,000-5,000 years old.

00:51:10.443 --> 00:51:11.790
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:51:11.790 --> 00:51:13.683
And I bought a seed there.

00:51:14.645 --> 00:51:16.050
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:51:16.050 --> 00:51:18.213
And the seed was very, very small.

00:51:19.191 --> 00:51:24.000
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:51:24.000 --> 00:51:26.370
I put it on top of my fingernails,

00:51:26.370 --> 00:51:30.551
and I rubbed it, and it was
just gone without a trace.

00:51:30.551 --> 00:51:35.218
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:51:36.510 --> 00:51:39.660
But imagine that great tree all started

00:51:39.660 --> 00:51:41.703
from the tiniest seeds.

00:51:43.238 --> 00:51:47.905
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:51:49.502 --> 00:51:53.116
When you feel worried, you
feel that you are very small.

00:51:53.116 --> 00:51:57.783
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:51:59.220 --> 00:52:01.950
But there is a great tree
waiting to grow inside of you,

00:52:01.950 --> 00:52:03.483
and you are that kind of seed.

00:52:04.367 --> 00:52:06.570
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:52:06.570 --> 00:52:07.743
Don't worry about it.

00:52:09.143 --> 00:52:11.640
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:52:11.640 --> 00:52:14.700
Please remember, go through
those trials and tribulations

00:52:14.700 --> 00:52:16.556
and keep exploring.

00:52:16.556 --> 00:52:19.380
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:52:19.380 --> 00:52:21.000
It is the best part of life,

00:52:21.000 --> 00:52:24.331
and it's great for you
to grow as a person.

00:52:24.331 --> 00:52:28.290
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:52:28.290 --> 00:52:31.170
I went through a lot of
trouble after being ordained,

00:52:31.170 --> 00:52:34.071
but that's made into who I am today.

00:52:34.071 --> 00:52:35.790
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:52:35.790 --> 00:52:37.863
I became a nun at 16.

00:52:39.138 --> 00:52:40.410
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:52:40.410 --> 00:52:42.993
What could I have known at that age?

00:52:44.125 --> 00:52:47.880
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:52:47.880 --> 00:52:49.620
I told them that I would bear

00:52:49.620 --> 00:52:51.573
whatever burden that comes my way.

00:52:59.970 --> 00:53:00.803
- Thank you.

00:53:03.030 --> 00:53:06.273
Hi, thank you again for coming
and speaking to us today.

00:53:07.350 --> 00:53:09.150
I also had a question about the game of,

00:53:09.150 --> 00:53:10.900
how you described the game of life.

00:53:11.850 --> 00:53:13.980
What would you say to
someone who might like

00:53:13.980 --> 00:53:15.270
be tired of the game of life

00:53:15.270 --> 00:53:17.320
and not really want to like keep playing?

00:53:19.290 --> 00:53:21.523
Yeah, I think that's it.

00:53:22.832 --> 00:53:27.665
(translator speaking in Korean)

00:53:30.233 --> 00:53:32.790
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:53:32.790 --> 00:53:35.193
- That is one decision you can make.

00:53:36.310 --> 00:53:40.977
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:53:48.090 --> 00:53:50.940
But if we have to make that
decision among all the options,

00:53:50.940 --> 00:53:52.980
wouldn't it be better to choose the option

00:53:52.980 --> 00:53:55.143
to keep continue playing that game?

00:53:56.638 --> 00:53:58.770
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:53:58.770 --> 00:54:03.065
Of course you might feel like
you don't want to do anything.

00:54:03.065 --> 00:54:04.920
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:54:04.920 --> 00:54:08.265
And it's okay not to do
something at the moment.

00:54:08.265 --> 00:54:10.110
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:54:10.110 --> 00:54:13.091
That's not a problem
when it comes to life.

00:54:13.091 --> 00:54:16.320
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:54:16.320 --> 00:54:20.073
Are you personally sort of
troubled by that kind of stuff?

00:54:21.131 --> 00:54:23.010
- No, one of my friends is.

00:54:23.010 --> 00:54:27.677
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:54:30.330 --> 00:54:32.280
- Would you be able to share

00:54:32.280 --> 00:54:35.100
more about that kind of feeling?

00:54:35.100 --> 00:54:39.840
- Yeah, I mean, like they
often tell me that it's like

00:54:39.840 --> 00:54:42.240
you like kind of go through things,

00:54:42.240 --> 00:54:44.310
and you don't really know
why you're doing everything.

00:54:44.310 --> 00:54:48.540
And like if it would
just be better to like

00:54:48.540 --> 00:54:50.340
stop playing the game of life, yeah.

00:54:52.073 --> 00:54:56.906
(translator speaking in Korean)

00:55:00.524 --> 00:55:05.430
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:55:05.430 --> 00:55:07.110
- There are a lot of young people nowadays

00:55:07.110 --> 00:55:09.311
who think the same things.

00:55:09.311 --> 00:55:10.144
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:55:10.144 --> 00:55:11.883
There are so many in Korea.

00:55:13.338 --> 00:55:18.338
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:55:18.780 --> 00:55:21.510
Perhaps we think about
those kinds of things

00:55:21.510 --> 00:55:24.603
because the world has become
a better place to live in.

00:55:25.913 --> 00:55:28.020
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:55:28.020 --> 00:55:31.032
There is no particular meaning to life.

00:55:31.032 --> 00:55:33.379
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:55:33.379 --> 00:55:34.833
We create our own meanings.

00:55:35.912 --> 00:55:40.353
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:55:40.353 --> 00:55:41.760
You need to be able to put in the effort

00:55:41.760 --> 00:55:43.263
to create that meaning.

00:55:45.016 --> 00:55:46.710
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:55:46.710 --> 00:55:49.110
And you want to help
your friend through this?

00:55:49.110 --> 00:55:50.010
- Yeah, of course.

00:55:54.408 --> 00:55:59.075
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:56:04.800 --> 00:56:07.380
- Sometimes that thing,
those kind of things arise

00:56:07.380 --> 00:56:10.623
out of a sense of
superiority or inferiority.

00:56:12.304 --> 00:56:17.160
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:56:17.160 --> 00:56:19.950
But in reality there's no
one who's like better than us

00:56:19.950 --> 00:56:21.783
or worse than us.

00:56:22.907 --> 00:56:27.810
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:56:27.810 --> 00:56:30.570
Does your friend take any
kind of like medication

00:56:30.570 --> 00:56:32.540
for that stuff?

00:56:32.540 --> 00:56:33.373
- No.

00:56:33.373 --> 00:56:34.206
- No, okay.

00:56:35.404 --> 00:56:37.170
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:56:37.170 --> 00:56:39.663
- Maybe you can suggest meditation.

00:56:41.525 --> 00:56:42.358
- Okay.

00:56:42.358 --> 00:56:43.260
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:56:43.260 --> 00:56:45.753
- Do you think they'll
be interested in that?

00:56:47.220 --> 00:56:48.870
- Honestly I don't think so, but.

00:56:49.830 --> 00:56:50.898
(translator speaking in Korean)

00:56:50.898 --> 00:56:53.610
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:56:53.610 --> 00:56:55.113
- What about sports?

00:56:56.127 --> 00:56:56.960
- No.

00:56:56.960 --> 00:57:00.321
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:57:00.321 --> 00:57:01.807
(audience laughs)

00:57:01.807 --> 00:57:03.673
- Then there's nothing I can say.

00:57:03.673 --> 00:57:08.340
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:57:14.400 --> 00:57:17.790
So you gave me a very good question,

00:57:17.790 --> 00:57:19.560
and I'd like to introduce a book

00:57:19.560 --> 00:57:21.963
by a Yale professor called Marc Brackett.

00:57:24.383 --> 00:57:27.180
- Permission to Feel.

00:57:27.180 --> 00:57:28.013
- Permission to Feel.

00:57:28.013 --> 00:57:31.186
- Permission to Feel, Marc Brackett.

00:57:31.186 --> 00:57:35.430
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:57:35.430 --> 00:57:38.370
- This is by Professor Marc
Brackett of Yale University,

00:57:38.370 --> 00:57:40.623
and it's also been translated into Korean.

00:57:41.544 --> 00:57:46.544
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:57:46.590 --> 00:57:48.720
I was very moved after reading this book,

00:57:48.720 --> 00:57:51.063
and I met the professor earlier today.

00:57:52.536 --> 00:57:57.203
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:57:59.640 --> 00:58:01.650
It hasn't been long
since we've been living

00:58:01.650 --> 00:58:03.360
with our emotions,

00:58:03.360 --> 00:58:05.520
and we've actually lived for a long time

00:58:05.520 --> 00:58:08.590
without feeling those kinds of emotions.

00:58:08.590 --> 00:58:11.970
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:58:11.970 --> 00:58:14.700
Apparently emotions as we know it

00:58:14.700 --> 00:58:19.345
have only been in existence
for the past 15,000 years.

00:58:19.345 --> 00:58:24.012
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:58:29.010 --> 00:58:33.060
Depression, anxiety, fear, all
these emotions are in reality

00:58:33.060 --> 00:58:36.179
what humans have created for themselves.

00:58:36.179 --> 00:58:38.100
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:58:38.100 --> 00:58:41.951
So that means we can also learn
to control those emotions.

00:58:41.951 --> 00:58:46.618
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:58:47.850 --> 00:58:51.900
And this book sort of explains
those kind of techniques

00:58:51.900 --> 00:58:54.030
that we can use to control those emotions,

00:58:54.030 --> 00:58:56.502
and it's been a great help to me.

00:58:56.502 --> 00:59:00.840
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:59:00.840 --> 00:59:03.690
I would very much like to
recommend this book to you all.

00:59:09.600 --> 00:59:12.663
- Thank you so much for
sharing your lovely story.

00:59:14.550 --> 00:59:18.810
You mentioned that we should
choose to grow vertically

00:59:18.810 --> 00:59:21.330
as opposed to horizontally as we age

00:59:21.330 --> 00:59:24.810
from infancy through old age.

00:59:24.810 --> 00:59:28.803
And my question to you is,
what are we growing towards?

00:59:29.910 --> 00:59:33.060
So to put it differently
in Buddhist teachings,

00:59:33.060 --> 00:59:38.060
what is considered a good
life before you end your life?

00:59:41.447 --> 00:59:46.280
(translator speaking in Korean)

00:59:50.898 --> 00:59:54.840
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

00:59:54.840 --> 00:59:57.903
- Have you studied or know
anything about Buddhism?

01:00:01.512 --> 01:00:04.984
- I'm not a practitioner, but
I am very much interested.

01:00:04.984 --> 01:00:06.891
(translator speaking in Korean)

01:00:06.891 --> 01:00:08.393
- Okay.

01:00:08.393 --> 01:00:13.060
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:00:14.280 --> 01:00:16.743
- So Buddhism suggests two things.

01:00:21.639 --> 01:00:26.306
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:00:28.560 --> 01:00:32.323
So what's the difference
between animals and humans

01:00:32.323 --> 01:00:35.340
is that we have that ability to perceive

01:00:35.340 --> 01:00:36.633
and understand things.

01:00:37.831 --> 01:00:42.360
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:00:42.360 --> 01:00:43.950
And that's why we put in the effort

01:00:43.950 --> 01:00:46.696
for our own personal growths.

01:00:46.696 --> 01:00:50.400
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:00:50.400 --> 01:00:52.530
And that's why we also help others

01:00:52.530 --> 01:00:54.845
to achieve their own growths.

01:00:54.845 --> 01:00:57.420
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:00:57.420 --> 01:01:00.300
So we learn about both of these things,

01:01:00.300 --> 01:01:02.160
learning from the growing for ourselves

01:01:02.160 --> 01:01:04.173
and helping others grow in Buddhism.

01:01:05.790 --> 01:01:10.790
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:01:11.070 --> 01:01:15.960
So this term grow is actually
used in English circles,

01:01:15.960 --> 01:01:18.213
and it's not a term
that we use in Buddhism.

01:01:19.187 --> 01:01:21.900
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:01:21.900 --> 01:01:23.520
And I just use the term growing

01:01:23.520 --> 01:01:25.953
to make it easier to understand.

01:01:26.910 --> 01:01:30.960
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:01:30.960 --> 01:01:34.923
In Buddha, we teach that each
of us are our own Buddhas.

01:01:35.920 --> 01:01:40.260
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:01:40.260 --> 01:01:43.050
Buddha isn't something
that exists outside.

01:01:43.050 --> 01:01:46.603
The Buddha is within all of us.

01:01:46.603 --> 01:01:49.110
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:01:49.110 --> 01:01:50.820
But we tend to think of ourselves

01:01:50.820 --> 01:01:52.323
and the Buddha as separate.

01:01:53.230 --> 01:01:55.020
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:01:55.020 --> 01:01:58.218
And so we continue looking for the Buddha.

01:01:58.218 --> 01:02:00.330
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:02:00.330 --> 01:02:04.440
But in reality we are the
Buddha sitting right here.

01:02:04.440 --> 01:02:05.273
- Okay.

01:02:10.680 --> 01:02:11.513
Finish it?

01:02:12.934 --> 01:02:13.767
Okay.

01:02:18.270 --> 01:02:20.806
- Thanks for all the sharing.

01:02:20.806 --> 01:02:23.220
I think I just have a simple question.

01:02:23.220 --> 01:02:26.820
So after practicing
Buddhism for so many years,

01:02:26.820 --> 01:02:29.583
what do you look forward to every day?

01:02:31.324 --> 01:02:36.157
(translator speaking in Korean)

01:02:40.459 --> 01:02:41.292
(Jeongmok laughs)

01:02:41.292 --> 01:02:43.803
- Very nice question, yes.

01:02:48.205 --> 01:02:50.910
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:02:50.910 --> 01:02:52.833
- So when I became a monk-

01:02:56.110 --> 01:03:01.110
(Jeongmok and translator
speaking in Korean)

01:03:19.230 --> 01:03:23.193
So I was very enamored about
the vows made in Buddhism.

01:03:24.783 --> 01:03:29.450
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:03:31.020 --> 01:03:33.870
And I was very enamored
by all the great teachings

01:03:33.870 --> 01:03:37.370
that I had never even seen in school.

01:03:37.370 --> 01:03:41.670
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:03:41.670 --> 01:03:44.040
So there is a vow in Buddhism where

01:03:44.040 --> 01:03:48.264
I want to be the medicine
for someone who is ill.

01:03:48.264 --> 01:03:52.931
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:03:54.442 --> 01:03:58.260
There is a vow that where
I want to become a bridge

01:03:58.260 --> 01:04:01.574
or a boat for someone who
is trying to cross a river.

01:04:01.574 --> 01:04:04.920
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:04:04.920 --> 01:04:07.953
So when I see these
vows, I feel very moved.

01:04:09.107 --> 01:04:12.958
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:04:12.958 --> 01:04:15.390
The fact that I can be
this kind of person,

01:04:15.390 --> 01:04:18.063
and the fact that I am
that kind of person.

01:04:19.104 --> 01:04:23.771
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:04:25.530 --> 01:04:27.150
So when I pray every morning,

01:04:27.150 --> 01:04:29.853
I pay my respects to
those great teachings.

01:04:30.852 --> 01:04:32.130
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:04:32.130 --> 01:04:33.873
To those beautiful teachings.

01:04:34.784 --> 01:04:36.556
- Okay.

01:04:36.556 --> 01:04:38.384
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

01:04:38.384 --> 01:04:39.217
- Okay.

01:04:39.217 --> 01:04:40.221
- [Audience Member] Thank you so much.

01:04:40.221 --> 01:04:42.750
(audience member speaking
in Korean)

01:04:42.750 --> 01:04:44.889
- [Audience Member] I
have one more question.

01:04:44.889 --> 01:04:46.076
- What did you say?

01:04:46.076 --> 01:04:47.346
Ah, yes.

01:04:47.346 --> 01:04:49.200
(audience member speaking
in Korean)

01:04:49.200 --> 01:04:52.839
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:04:52.839 --> 01:04:55.080
- Okay, I don't know,
maybe I can, you know,

01:04:55.080 --> 01:04:56.970
explain exactly or not,

01:04:56.970 --> 01:04:59.070
but yeah, I'm Korean,

01:04:59.070 --> 01:05:03.300
as moved to the here little
bit more than 20 years ago.

01:05:03.300 --> 01:05:06.420
When I came here I saw your profile.

01:05:06.420 --> 01:05:11.130
Then one of your, the
name of the book More,

01:05:12.877 --> 01:05:17.877
"Getting More Empty,
You Can Get More Full,"

01:05:17.910 --> 01:05:19.293
something like that, right?

01:05:21.634 --> 01:05:26.634
(audience member speaking
in Korean)

01:06:01.862 --> 01:06:02.718
- Okay.

01:06:02.718 --> 01:06:05.100
- So the question was about
how do you empty yourself.

01:06:05.100 --> 01:06:09.663
Because when he read one of Sumim's books,

01:06:10.530 --> 01:06:13.380
and he was very captivated by the title

01:06:13.380 --> 01:06:16.050
because the title is "The More You Empty,

01:06:16.050 --> 01:06:17.910
The Fuller it Gets."

01:06:17.910 --> 01:06:20.730
And when he received his
Dharma name in Korea,

01:06:20.730 --> 01:06:22.680
his dharma name means emptiness.

01:06:22.680 --> 01:06:25.410
And so he tried reading
the flower Garland Sutra,

01:06:25.410 --> 01:06:27.120
but it was too hard,
and he couldn't read it,

01:06:27.120 --> 01:06:30.573
and he still doesn't really
know how to empty yourself.

01:06:31.529 --> 01:06:32.610
And the question was about that.

01:06:32.610 --> 01:06:33.443
- Okay.

01:06:35.825 --> 01:06:39.330
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:06:39.330 --> 01:06:41.220
- Have you heard of the Gestalt theory?

01:06:41.220 --> 01:06:42.123
- No, okay.

01:06:43.595 --> 01:06:47.670
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:06:47.670 --> 01:06:49.800
- So in the night sky, when we see stars,

01:06:49.800 --> 01:06:51.693
we say that the stars are twinkling.

01:06:52.932 --> 01:06:57.021
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:06:57.021 --> 01:06:58.980
But in reality we're not
looking at the stars,

01:06:58.980 --> 01:07:01.353
we're looking at a dark night sky.

01:07:02.440 --> 01:07:05.490
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:07:05.490 --> 01:07:08.943
We need that dark night sky
in order to see the stars.

01:07:09.780 --> 01:07:12.450
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:07:12.450 --> 01:07:15.183
You can't separate the
stars from the dark sky.

01:07:16.184 --> 01:07:18.210
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:07:18.210 --> 01:07:21.197
And there is hatred because there is love.

01:07:21.197 --> 01:07:22.530
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:07:22.530 --> 01:07:26.769
And there are farewells
because there are encounters.

01:07:26.769 --> 01:07:28.590
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:07:28.590 --> 01:07:31.563
We have this because
there is that and other.

01:07:32.477 --> 01:07:33.310
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:07:33.310 --> 01:07:35.211
And that is Buddhism.

01:07:35.211 --> 01:07:36.630
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:07:36.630 --> 01:07:39.003
And to know that is to know emptiness.

01:07:39.904 --> 01:07:42.264
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:07:42.264 --> 01:07:44.320
- Is this-
(audience laughing)

01:07:44.320 --> 01:07:46.368
(Jeongmok speaking in Korean)

01:07:46.368 --> 01:07:47.291
- Yes, thank you.

01:07:47.291 --> 01:07:50.594
- On that note, please give
her a big round of applause.

01:07:50.594 --> 01:07:53.761
(audience applauding)

01:07:56.880 --> 01:07:58.346
- Thank you so much.

01:07:58.346 --> 01:07:59.179
Thank you.

01:08:00.599 --> 01:08:03.182
(gentle music)

